    "lawCode": "Add §837-ff, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2056\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the executive law and the public health law, in\nrelation to missing cognitively impaired citizens\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo enact a Silver Alert System for New York State.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 adds \"missing cognitively impaired citizen\" to the Amber\nAlert statute.\n\nSection 2 adds new language to subdivision 4 of section 2004-a of the\nPublic Health Law, authoring the Coordinating council for services\nrelated to Alzheimer's disease and other dementia to study the use of\nlocator technology for missing cognitively impaired citizens.\n\nSection 3 is a severability clause.\n\nSection 4 establishes an effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nLast year, a Syracuse woman who suffers from Alzheimer's disease left\nher home in the middle of the night and, though the help of several\ngood Samaritans, traveled all the way to her former home in New Haven,\nConnecticut. Medical authorities were only alerted after she\ndiscovered someone living in her former house and became disoriented.\nThis incident and others like it demonstrate the need for a \"Silver\nAlert\" system for vulnerable senior citizens similar to the \"Amber\nAlert\" system. By enacting this law, New York would join several other\nstates, including Illinois, West Virginia, North Carolina, and Texas,\nin taking steps to assist families of senior citizens with locating\ntheir missing loved ones.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nSimilar to A10265B of 2007-2008.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nTo be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nOn the one hundred twentieth day after it shall have become law.\nEffective immediately, the addition, amendment and/or repeal of any\nrule or regulation necessary for the implementation of this act on its\neffective date are authorized and directed to be made and completed on\nor before such effective date.\n",
